# How to write code here

## Before writing
- Restate the exact behaviour you will implement, including edge cases, in a
  few lines. If the request says "like X" and also describes X, and the two
  disagree, say so and ask -- do not silently pick one.
- Do not trust your memory of a library's edge-case behaviour. If the real
  implementation is available (the old code, the stdlib, the dependency),
  run it on the edge cases and use what it returns.

## Rewrites, ports, refactors: the old code is the oracle
When replacing code that already works, or reproducing a library function:
1. Write a differential test that calls the reference and the new code on
   - at least 15 hand-written edge cases, each a DIFFERENT behaviour, and
   - at least 300 seeded random inputs (`random.Random(0)`).
   Keep the two groups separate. Compare exceptions by type. Guard every call.
   Print every failing edge case, then the first few random failures, then
   `MISMATCHES n OF total EDGE e OF edges`.
2. Validate the test first: run it with the reference in place of the new
   code. It must report 0 mismatches. If it does not, the TEST is wrong --
   usually swapped arguments or comparing exception objects with `==`.
3. Only then fix the new code against it.

## Tests are the spec, not an obstacle
- Never weaken, delete or rewrite a test to make code pass. Change a test only
  when it is provably wrong, and prove it: the reference implementation (or
  the documented behaviour) must still pass the changed test, with no fewer
  cases. State that you changed a test and why.
- Tests you wrote yourself catch slips (one wrong line), not
  misunderstandings: if you misread the spec, your tests share the mistake.
  For behaviour-heavy code, check at least the trickiest edge cases against
  something you did not write.

## Fixing failures
- Count distinct failing behaviours, not failing cases. One bug can fail
  hundreds of random inputs; fixing it is not worth breaking a rarer case.
- After every fix, re-run the WHOLE edge list. A fix that makes a previously
  passing edge case fail is a regression: revert it.
- Two attempts at the same failure without progress means the approach is
  wrong. Stop patching: trace the failing input through the code step by
  step, or rewrite the function from the spec.
- In parsers and state machines, check the slips that recur: a buffer not
  reset between items, a quote/escape state not closed, an index advanced
  twice, the last item not flushed at end of input.

## Working style
- Write the code, run it, read the result. Do not deliberate at length over
  edge cases in prose -- run them.
- Small, complete changes. Show the command you ran and its real output;
  never describe output you have not seen.
